<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|<tuple|tmdoc|english>>

<\body>
  <tmdoc-title|Getting help while you work>

  <TeXmacs> gives you a lot of information while you work, which makes it
  easy to discover its features one at a time.

  <paragraph*|Tooltips>

  When you leave the mouse pointer on an icon for a moment, a small
  <em|tooltip> (also called <em|help balloon>) appears. It explains what the
  icon does, and often also the keyboard shortcut which does the same thing.
  For instance, the tooltip of the <icon|tm_copy.png> icon tells you that it
  copies the selected text and that the same is obtained with
  <shortcut|(kbd-copy)>.

  <paragraph*|Keyboard shortcuts in the menus>

  Most menu entries also show their keyboard shortcut at their right. For
  instance, the entry <menu|File|Save> shows the shortcut
  <shortcut|(save-buffer)>. After a while, you will use these shortcuts
  instead of the menus for the actions which you perform most often.

  <paragraph*|The status bar>

  The left part of the status bar also guides you while you type a command
  which takes several keystrokes. For instance, when you type a symbol in a
  mathematical formula, it shows you which keys led to the current symbol,
  and while you type a command after the <key|\\> key, it tells you what will
  happen when you press <key|return>. We will meet many examples of this in
  the following chapters.

  <paragraph*|The help menu>

  Finally, the <menu|Help> menu gives you access to the full documentation
  of <TeXmacs>. <menu|Help|Welcome> and <menu|Help|Getting started> are short
  introductions, <menu|Help|Manual> contains the user manual, sorted by
  topics, and <menu|Help|Search|Documentation> allows you to search the documentation for
  a<nbsp>word.

